== 8. The Great Prevailing Power of Secret Prayer

Eighthly, consider the great prevailing power of secret prayer. Private prayer is #emph[porta coeli, clavis paradisi], the gate of heaven, a key to let us into Paradise. Oh, the great things that private prayer has done with God (Ps 31:22)! Oh, the great mercies that have been obtained by private prayer (Ps 38:8–9)! And oh, the great threatenings that have been turned aside by private prayer! And oh, the great judgments that have been removed by private prayer! And oh, the great judgments that have been prevented by private prayer! I have read of a malicious woman who gave herself to the devil on condition that he would do harm to a certain neighbor whom she hated with a deadly hatred. The devil went again and again to do his errand, but at last he returned and told her that he could do that man no hurt, for whenever he came, he found him either reading the Scriptures or at private prayer. Private prayers pierce the heavens, and are usually blessed and loaded with gracious and glorious returns from there. While Hezekiah was praying and weeping in private, God sent the prophet Isaiah to him to assure him that his prayer was heard, and that his tears were seen, and that He would add fifteen years to his days (Isa 38:5). So, when Isaac was all alone in the fields, meditating and praying and pleading with God for a good wife, he meets Rebekah (Gen 24:63–64).

So it was with Jacob: “So Jacob was left all alone, and there a man wrestled with him until daybreak. When the man saw that he could not prevail against Jacob, he struck the socket of Jacob’s hip and dislocated it as they wrestled. Then the man said, ‘Let me go, for it is daybreak.’ But Jacob replied, ‘I will not let you go unless you bless me.’ ‘What is your name?’ the man asked. ‘Jacob,’ he replied. Then the man said, ‘Your name will no longer be Jacob, but Israel, for as a prince you have power with God and with men, and you have prevailed’” (Gen 32:24–28). In this scripture we have an elegant description of a duel fought between the Almighty and Jacob, and in it these things are most worth observing:

=== a. The Combatants: Jacob, and God in the Appearance of a Man

(1) First, we have the combatants or duelists, Jacob and God, who appeared in the shape or appearance of a man. He who is here said to be a man was the Son of God in human shape, as appears from the whole narrative, and from Hosea 12:3–5. Now, that this man who wrestled with Jacob was indeed God, and not really a man, is most evident for these reasons:

#emph[First, Jacob desires a blessing from Him] (v. 26). Now, it is God’s royal prerogative to bless, not that of angels or men.

#emph[Secondly, he calls Him by the name of God]. “As a prince you have power with God” (v. 28). And Jacob says, “I have seen God face to face” (v. 30). Not that he saw the majesty and essence of God, for no man can see the essential glory of God and live (Exod 33:20, 23); but he saw God more plainly, more manifestly, more gloriously than he ever had before. Jacob saw some created shape, some glimpse of glory, by which God was pleased for the time to show His more immediate presence; but he did not see God Himself.

#emph[Thirdly, the same person that Jacob wrestles with here is He Whom Jacob remembers in his blessing as his deliverer from all evil] (Gen 48:16). It was the God who appeared to him at Bethel “as he fled from his brother” (Gen 35:7).

#emph[Fourthly, Jacob is reproved for his prying inquiry] or asking after the Angel’s name (v. 29), which is a clear argument or demonstration of His majesty and glory, God being above all notion and name. God is a substance above all substance, an understanding not to be understood, a word never to be spoken. One man, asked what God was, answered that he would have to be God Himself before he could know God fully. We are as able to hold the sea in a cockleshell as we are to comprehend the Almighty, or that #emph[nomen majestativum] (majestic name), as Tertullian puts it. “In searching after God,” says Chrysostom, “I am like a man digging in a deep spring: I stand here, and the water rises on me; and I stand there, and still the water rises on me.”

In this conflict you#super[+] have not one man wrestling with another, nor a man wrestling with a created angel, but a poor, weak, mortal man wrestling with an immortal God: weakness wrestling with strength, and a finite being with an infinite being. Though Jacob had no second, though he was all alone, though he was overmatched beyond measure, yet he wrestles and keeps his hold, and all in the strength of the One he wrestles with.

=== b. The Place: Beside the Ford Jabbok

Secondly, you#super[+] have the place where they fought, and that was beside the ford Jabbok (Gen 32:22). This is the name of a brook or river rising by Rabba, the chief city of the Ammonites, and flowing into the Jordan below the Sea of Galilee (Num 21:24; Deut 2:37; Judg 11:13, 15; Deut 3:16). Jacob never enjoyed so much of the presence of God as when he had left the company of men. Oh, the sweet communion that Jacob had with God when he had withdrawn from his family and was all alone with his God by the ford Jabbok! Certainly, Jacob was never less alone than at this time, when he was so alone. Saints often meet with the best wine and the strongest heart-medicines when they are all alone with God.

=== c. The Time: The Night, and It Lasted Until Break of Day

Thirdly, you#super[+] have the time of the combat, and that was the night. At what time of the night this wrestling, this duel, began, we do not read anywhere. But it lasted until break of day; it lasted until Jacob had got the better of the Angel. How many hours of the night this conflict lasted, no mortal man can tell. God’s design was that no one but Jacob should be a spectator or witness of this combat; and therefore Jacob had to be wrestling while others were sleeping.

=== d. The Ground of the Quarrel: Jacob’s Fear of Esau, and His Desire for a Blessing

Fourthly, you#super[+] have the ground of the quarrel, and that was Jacob’s fear of Esau, and his importunate desire for a blessing. Jacob flees to God so that he would not fall before man; he flees to God so that he would not flee before men. In a storm there is no shelter like the wing of God. He who places himself under divine protection is safest and happiest and wisest. Jacob knew this, and so he runs to God as to his only city of refuge. In this conflict God was ready to break off: “Let me go, for it is daybreak” (v. 26); but Jacob keeps his hold and tells Him boldly to His very face that he would not let Him go unless He blessed him. Oh, the power of private prayer! It has a kind of omnipotence in it. It takes God captive; it holds Him as a prisoner; it binds the hands of the Almighty; indeed, it will wring a mercy, a blessing, out of the hand of heaven itself. Oh, the power of that prayer that makes a man victorious over the greatest, the highest power! Jacob was a man, a single man, a traveling man, a tired man; indeed, he was a worm that is easily crushed and trodden underfoot, and no man (Isa 41:14). Yet in private prayer he is so potent that he overcomes the omnipotent God; he is so mighty that he overcomes the Almighty.

=== e. The Nature of the Combat: Both Outward and Inward, Bodily and Spiritual

Fifthly, you#super[+] have the nature or manner of the combat, and that was both outward and inward, both bodily and spiritual. It was by might and skill; it was as much by the strength of his body as by the force of his faith. He wrestled not only with spiritual strugglings, tears, and prayers (Hos 12:4), but with bodily ones too, in which God assailed him with one hand and upheld him with the other. In this conflict Jacob and the Angel of the covenant really did lock arm with arm, set shoulder to shoulder, and put foot to foot, and used all the other holds and tricks that men use when they wrestle with one another. The Hebrew word ואבק, from אבק, which is here rendered #emph[wrestled], signifies the raising of the dust. For those who wrestled in old times not only wrestled naked, as the custom then was, but also used to throw dust on one another, so that they might take surer hold of one another. Some conclude from this word #emph[abak] that Jacob and the Angel tugged, strove, and turned each other until they were dripping with sweat, for so much the word implies. Jacob and the Angel did not wrestle in jest, but in good earnest; they wrestled with all their might, as it were, for the victor’s garland; they strove for victory as for life.

But as this wrestling was bodily, so it was spiritual also. Jacob’s soul takes hold of God, Jacob’s faith takes hold of God, Jacob’s prayers take hold of God, and Jacob’s tears take hold of God (Hos 12:4–5). Certainly, Jacob’s weapons in this warfare were mainly spiritual, and so “mighty through God” (2 Cor 10:4). There is no overcoming God except in His own strength. Jacob did more by his royal faith than by his noble hands, more by weeping than by sweating, and more by praying than by all his bodily strivings.

=== f. The Issue of the Combat: Victory over the Angel

Sixthly and lastly, you#super[+] have the outcome of the combat, and that is victory over the Angel (v. 28). Jacob wrestles in the Angel’s arms and armor, and so overcomes Him. As a prince, he overpowers the Angel by the very power he had from the Angel. The Angel was as freely and fully willing to be conquered by Jacob as Jacob was willing to be the conqueror. When lovers wrestle, the stronger is willing enough to take a fall from the weaker; and so it was here. The father, wrestling with his child, is willing enough, for his child’s comfort and encouragement, to take a fall now and then; and so it was between the Angel and Jacob in the present case. Now in this blessed story, as in a crystal mirror, you#super[+] may see the great power, the prevailing power, of private prayer. It conquers the great Conqueror; it is so omnipotent that it overcomes an omnipotent God.

Now you#super[+] may see this cleared up more fully and sweetly in Hosea 12:3–4: “In the womb he grasped his brother’s heel, and by his strength he had power with God. Yes, he had power over the angel and prevailed; he wept and made supplication to Him; he found Him at Bethel and spoke with Him there.” When Jacob was all alone, and in a dark night, and standing on only one leg, even then he played the prince with God, as the Hebrew has it. Jacob, by prayers and tears, so played the prince with God that he carried off the blessing. Jacob’s wrestling was by weeping, and his prevailing by praying. Prayers and tears are not only very pleasing to God, but also very prevailing with God. And so you#super[+] see that this great instance of Jacob proclaims out loud the prevailing power of private prayer.

See another instance of this in David: “I am weary from groaning; all night I flood my bed with weeping and drench my couch with tears” (Ps 6:6). These are all hyperbolic figures of speech, to set forth the greatness of his sorrow and the multitude of his tears. David in his seclusion makes the place of his sin, his bed, the place of his repentance. David sins privately on his bed, and David mourns privately on his bed. Every place that we have polluted by sin, we should sanctify and water with our tears. “Depart from me, all you#super[+] workers of iniquity, for the #smallcaps[Lord] has heard the voice of my weeping” (Ps 6:8). As blood has a voice, and as the rod has a voice, so tears have a voice. Tears have tongues, and tears can speak. No noise compares with the noise that tears in secret make in the ears of God. A wise and tender father can better make out the needs of his children by their secret tears than by their loud complaints, by their weeping than by their words; and do you#super[+] think that God cannot do as much? Tears are not always mute (Lam 2:18). “Cry aloud,” says one, “not with your tongue, but with your eyes; not with your words, but with your tears; for that is the prayer that makes the most forcible entry into the ears of the great God of heaven.” Penitent tears are ambassadors that cannot be denied, and that never return from the throne of grace without a gracious answer. Tears are a kind of silent prayer: though they say nothing, yet they obtain pardon; and though they do not plead a man’s cause, yet they obtain mercy at the hands of God. You#super[+] see this in that great instance of Peter, who, though he said nothing that we read of, yet by weeping bitterly obtained mercy (Matt 26:75). I have read of Augustine, who, coming as a visitor to the house of a sick man, saw the room full of friends and kindred, all silent, yet all weeping: the wife sobbing, the children sighing, the kinsfolk lamenting, all mourning. At this Augustine uttered this short, sudden prayer: “Lord, what prayers do You hear, if not these?” “The #smallcaps[Lord] has heard my cry for mercy; the #smallcaps[Lord] receives my prayer” (Ps 6:9). God sometimes answers His people before they pray: “Even before they call, I will answer” (Isa 65:24). And sometimes while they are praying; so it follows in the same verse: “and while they are still speaking, I will hear.” So Isa 30:19 says: “He will surely be gracious when you cry for help; when He hears, He will answer you.” And sometimes after they have prayed, as the experience of all Christians can testify. Sometimes God neither hears nor receives a prayer, and this is the common case and lot of the wicked (Prov 1:28; Job 27:9; Isa 1:15). Sometimes God hears the prayers of His people, but does not answer them at once, as in the case of Paul (2 Cor 12:7–9). And sometimes God both hears and receives the prayers of His people, as He did David’s here. Now in this instance of David, as in a mirror, you#super[+] may read at a glance the prevailing power of private prayer and of secret tears.

You#super[+] may take another instance of this in Jonah: “From the belly of the fish, Jonah prayed to the #smallcaps[Lord] his God, saying: ‘In my distress I called to the #smallcaps[Lord], and He answered me. From the belly of hell I called for help, and You heard my voice. For You cast me into the deep, into the heart of the seas, and the current swirled about me; all Your breakers and waves swept over me. … The waters engulfed me to take my life; the watery depths closed around me; the seaweed wrapped around my head. … As my life was fading away, I remembered the #smallcaps[Lord]. My prayer went up to You, to Your holy temple.’ … And the #smallcaps[Lord] commanded the fish, and it vomited Jonah onto dry land” (Jonah 2:1–3, 5, 7, 10). When Jonah was all alone, and in the midst of many dangers and deaths; when he was in the whale’s belly, indeed, in the belly of hell (so called because it was horrid and hideous, deep and dismal), yet even then private prayer fetches him out of it. Let a man’s dangers be ever so many and ever so great, yet secret prayer has a certain omnipotence in it that will deliver him out of them all. In multiplied afflictions, private prayer prevails most with God. In the very midst of drowning, secret prayer will keep both head and heart above water. On Jonah’s private prayer, God sends out His writ of command, and the fish serves Jonah for a ship to sail safely to shore. When the case is quite desperate, even then private prayer can do much with God. Private prayer has such power that it can open the doors of leviathan, as you#super[+] see in this great instance, though that is reckoned a thing that cannot be done (Job 41:14).

You#super[+] have another instance of the prevailing power of private prayer in 2 Kings 4:32–33: “When Elisha reached the house, there was the boy lying dead on his bed. So he went in, shut the door behind the two of them, and prayed to the #smallcaps[Lord].” Privacy is a good help to fervency in prayer. “Then Elisha got on the bed and lay on the boy, mouth to mouth, eye to eye, and hand to hand. As he stretched himself out over him, the boy’s body became warm. Elisha turned away and paced back and forth across the room. Then he got on the bed and stretched himself out over the boy again, and the boy sneezed seven times and opened his eyes” (vv. 34–35). Oh, the power, the prevailing power, the omnipotence of private prayer, that raises the dead to life! And the private prayer of Elijah had the same effect in raising the son of the widow of Zarephath to life (1 Kgs 17:18ff). The great prevailing power of Moses’ private prayers you#super[+] may read in the following scriptures: “Soon the people began to complain about their hardship in the hearing of the #smallcaps[Lord], and when He heard them, His anger was kindled, and fire from the #smallcaps[Lord] blazed among them and consumed the outskirts of the camp. And the people cried out to Moses, and he prayed to the #smallcaps[Lord], and the fire died down” (Num 11:1–2). Moses by private prayer rules and overrules with God; he was so powerful with God in private prayer that he could have what he wanted from God (see also Num 21:7–9; Ps 106:23; Exod 32:9–14; Exod 14:15–17). You#super[+] may see the same in Nehemiah 1:11 compared with 2:4–8. So Luther, seeing the cause of God and the work of reformation greatly strained and in danger, went into his closet, and never stopped wrestling with God until he had received a gracious answer from heaven. On this he came out of his closet to his friends, leaping and triumphing, with #emph[vicimus, vicimus], “We have overcome, we have overcome,” in his mouth. It is observed that at that very time a proclamation came out from Charles the Fifth that no one should be harassed any further for the profession of the gospel. At another time, Luther, being in private prayer for a sick friend of his who was a great comfort and help to him, had a particular answer that he would recover. On this he was so confident that he sent word to his friend that he would certainly recover; and so it turned out. And so Latimer prayed with great zeal for three things: 1) that Queen Elizabeth might come to the crown; 2) that he might seal the truth with his heart’s blood; and 3) that the gospel might be restored once again, once again, which he expressed with great vehemence of spirit. God heard him in all three.

Constantine commanded that his image should be engraved, not like other emperors, leaning in their armor, but in a posture of prayer, kneeling, to show the world that he won more by secret prayer than by open battles.

Mr. Dod reports that many good people had often sought the Lord on behalf of a woman who was possessed by the devil, and yet could not prevail. At last they appointed a day for fasting and prayer, and at that time a poor woman came to the door of the room where the service had begun and asked to come in; but because she was poor they would not let her in. On that, the poor woman knelt behind the door and sought God by prayer. But she had not prayed long before the evil spirit raged, roared, and cried out in the possessed woman, “Take away the old woman behind the door, for I must be gone; take away the old woman behind the door, for I must be gone.” And so, by the old woman’s prayers behind the door, he was cast out. Oh, the prevailing power of prayer behind the door! And so you#super[+] see by all these great instances the great prevailing power of private prayer.

Like Saul’s sword and Jonathan’s bow, private prayer never returns empty when both the person who prays and the act of praying are as they should be. It hits the mark; it carries the day with God. It pierces the walls of heaven, though they are made of brass and iron like those of Gaza (Isa 45:2). Oh, who can express the powerful oratory of private prayer!
